data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1792316804760,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed mission of NASA's Apollo program.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard CM Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong declared 'That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind' upon stepping onto the lunar surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples led to identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years afterwards.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Saturn V\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"United States\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, which surprised the world and fueled fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority and sparking the Space Race.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Soviet cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and the first to orbit the Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On May 5, 1961, Alan Shepard became the first American in space, on a 15-minute suborbital flight rather than a full orbit.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, succeeding Eisenhower, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive or important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said that sending a man to the Moon would be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put him there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University's football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"In the speech, Kennedy said outer space had no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards threaten everyone alike.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy asked why the Moon was chosen as the goal, comparing it to climbing the highest mountain, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"The chunk's refrain ends with Kennedy declaring 'We choose to go to the Moon'.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"outer space\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy's 1962 speech framed going to the Moon as a hard challenge worth accepting, one the nation intended to win.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The Moon landing effort was already named Project Apollo when Kennedy pursued it.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing project to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy repeated the joint lunar expedition proposal at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the idea was abandoned after his death.\"},{\"id\":\"7.6\",\"text\":\"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module had a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"The lunar orbit rendezvous design meant Apollo could be launched by a single Saturn V rocket then under development.\"},{\"id\":\"8.6\",\"text\":\"Apollo technologies and techniques were developed by Project Gemini; NASA adopted MOSFETs and silicon IC chips enabling Apollo.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and led to an investigation that abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Apollo spacecraft\",\"command module\",\"service module\",\"Lunar Module\",\"Eagle\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"outer space\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"direct ascent\",\"Earth orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"command module (CM)\",\"service module (SM)\",\"lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"96bcd11c022826846531d7ce3c8f09465316084922d86f593b104f2f995339ec"}}

